## Question briefing

**CONSTRAINT:** When answer type is ordinary sentences, reply shape is ordinary skim, the reply asks the human a question, and the reply is longer than two or three sentences, MUST brief that ask before `### Question`. MUST NOT dump nearby objects at equal depth. MUST NOT apply this briefing on failed-run chronology, flow-sketch, or source-pointer shapes, or on explain-implementation channels.

MUST open with what this reply is about (the decision on the table) and the expected outcome of yes, in cold-read nouns a reader who has only this message can follow. MUST NOT open with how to read the message, a section menu, or `Skip unless`.

Then sections, in this order, with silent jobs (MUST NOT print the job names `Related`, `Proof`, or `Environment`):

1. Related to the Question (facts that change the yes/no)
2. Proof that earns a related claim, omitted when the related section already holds that fact
3. Environment that is not this ask, omitted when there is none
4. `### Question`

Headings MUST be cold-read claims: a reader who has only this message MUST know the job from the heading plus its first sentence. MUST NOT use a nickname (`the list`, `sharing`, `the completeness check`, `this close`) until the same message has named the thing in spoken English. MUST NOT use a heading that only names a bin (`The rest is not this session`) or an object tour (`Two PRs are waiting`).

Before placing each fact, complete this gate in working memory:

```text
Candidate: [fact]
Bears on the Question yes/no: YES: [quote] / NO
Proves a related claim already made: YES: [quote] / NO
Nearby work that is not this ask: YES: [quote] / NO
Decision: opener | related | proof | environment | cut
```

IF opener → the decision on the table, or what yes produces. MUST NOT restate the Question sentence.
IF related → a heading that names the job.
IF proof → only when a related claim needs a second fact.
IF environment → after related and proof; the heading MUST say this work is not the ask.
IF cut → omit.

Before sending each paragraph or heading, complete this gate in working memory:

```text
Candidate: [paragraph or heading]
Already said by a heading or the opener: YES: [quote] / NO
